11. Reporting findings
A useful report should include:
1. Title: State the workflow and the race-condition class.
2. Summary: Explain the violated invariant in plain language.
3. Affected component: Identify the relevant endpoint(s) and
   workflow.
4. Preconditions: Describe the account state, permissions, and
   required setup.
5. Reproduction: Provide the smallest sequential baseline and
   concurrent test procedure.
6. Evidence: Include sanitized requests/responses and the
   final-state or side-effect proof.
7. Reliability: State how often the behavior reproduced and any
   relevant limitations.
8. Impact: Describe the demonstrated security or business-rule
   consequence without speculation.
9. Remediation direction: Explain the state transition or
   authorization check that must be made safe.
Defensive remediation ideas
The right fix depends on the root cause, but common approaches include:
- Enforce authorization and MFA completion on every sensitive
  server-side operation.
- Avoid exposing partially authenticated sessions; represent
  authentication state explicitly.
- Make security-critical state transitions atomic where possible.
- Use database transactions, appropriate locking, uniqueness
  constraints, or compare-and-swap/version checks when suitable for
  the data model.
- Make one-time operations genuinely single-use and enforce that
  property at the data layer.
- Ensure validation and the consequential state change cannot be
  separated by an unsafe race window.
- Add concurrency-focused regression tests for the invariant, not only
  for one observed request sequence.
A UI redirect or client-side check is not a substitute for server-side
enforcement.
